{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"insane-poetic-borer","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\nThe sandbox enforcement tests added by `e668-b496-e264-4283` in\n`tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py` assert only that a file is ABSENT after\nrunning a wrapped subprocess:\n\n```python\nassert not target.exists()          # denial \"proven\"\n```\n\nNothing asserts the sandboxed child actually launched and reached the write attempt.\nIf `sandbox-exec`/`bwrap` fails to start at all — bad profile, missing binary, a\nnamespace refusal, an argv regression — the write never happens, the file is absent,\nand the test PASSES. A total failure of the sandbox wrapper is indistinguishable from\ncorrect enforcement.\n\n`test_the_incident_shape_rm_rf_is_denied` has the same shape in reverse: it asserts a\npre-existing `keep.txt` still exists. A sandbox launch failure trivially leaves the file\nin place, producing a false pass.\n\n## Why this matters more than a normal test gap\n\nThese are the ONLY tests that demonstrate the sandbox works. `e668`'s acceptance\ncriteria deliberately required observed enforcement rather than argv assertions,\nprecisely so the control could not be satisfied by construction alone. A test that\npasses when the mechanism is absent silently reintroduces the weakness those criteria\nwere written to exclude — the evidence for the whole feature rests on it.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Every enforcement test asserts positive LIVENESS of the sandboxed child: the\n      child ran and reached the write attempt (e.g. a sentinel written to an ALLOWED\n      path in the same command, or an asserted exit code / stderr signature that only\n      an executed-and-denied child produces).\n- [ ] The liveness assertion is proven load-bearing: with the sandbox mechanism forced\n      unavailable so the child never launches, the test goes RED rather than green.\n      Demonstrated by real substitution, not asserted.\n- [ ] `test_the_incident_shape_rm_rf_is_denied` gains the same liveness check, so a\n      launch failure cannot pass as \"the target survived\".\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- No enforcement test in the file can pass when the sandbox fails to launch.\n- Each liveness check is shown RED under a forced launch failure.\n- `make lint`, `make typecheck`.\n\n## Notes\n\nFound by the Gerrit `LLM-Review` bot on change 2256 (patch sets 4 and 7; advisory, so\nit did not block the merge). Discovered from `e668-b496-e264-4283`.","id":"c100-c4f1-2a2b-4905","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Sandbox enforcement tests pass when the sandbox fails to launch"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787855605495328001,"uuid":"76ad2c97-8cf2-4d97-8561-45af6b55a4e8"}